      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#f4f1ea"
      },
      "components": [
        {
          "id": "body",
          "type": "text",
          "enabled": true,
          "order": 0,
          "props": {
            "body": "ConfigMesh treats configuration the way every other engineering discipline treats its inputs: as typed, validated, version-controlled artifacts that fail at the boundary, not in production. You declare a schema once. Every service in your mesh reads that schema. Mismatches surface at boot — not in a Sentry alert at 3 AM after a customer churned. The runtime emits an AISP-compatible spec on every reload, so your config layer is round-trippable from human prose to deployed state with under 2% ambiguity. That's the same spec discipline Hey Bradley applies to its build pipelines, and it's why our users stop having config-drift incidents within two weeks of adoption. The migration from process.env to ConfigMesh in a 30-service monorepo takes a senior engineer about four hours. We've watched it happen at three Fortune-500 companies. The thing they all say afterward: 'I can't believe we lived without this.'"
          }
        }
      ],
      "content": {
        "heading": "Why ConfigMesh",
        "subheading": "A typed contract for the most ignored layer of your stack"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "columns",
      "id": "features-01",
      "enabled": true,
      "order": 2,
      "variant": "glass",
      "layout": {
        "display": "grid",
        "columns": 3,
        "gap": "24px",
        "padding": "64px 24px"
